> domain (x^2-1)/(x-1)
domain of (x^2 - 1)/(x - 1):
  x: real (default [-10, 10])
  requires x - 1 != 0  (in (x^2 - 1)/(x - 1))
  defined on (numerically, grid of 20001 over [-10, 10]):
    x in [-10, 1)
    x in (1, 10]
    except x ≈ 1  (x - 1 = 0)
> continuity (x^2-1)/(x-1)
continuity of (x^2 - 1)/(x - 1):
  undefined at x = 1 (the ends located numerically)
  x = 1: removable: both limits are 2, f is undefined there
  proved: built from operations that are continuous where defined, so it is continuous on its domain; points outside it located numerically; x has no range: searched [-10, 10]
> domain sqrt(x)
domain of sqrt(x):
  x: real (default [-10, 10])
  requires x >= 0
  defined on (numerically, grid of 20001 over [-10, 10]):
    x in [0, 10]
> domain 1/sqrt(x)
domain of 1/sqrt(x):
  x: real (default [-10, 10])
  requires sqrt(x) != 0  (in 1/sqrt(x))
  requires x >= 0
  defined on (numerically, grid of 20001 over [-10, 10]):
    x in (0, 10]
    except x ≈ 0  (sqrt(x) = 0)
> domain log(x)/(x-1)
domain of log(x)/(x - 1):
  x: real (default [-10, 10])
  requires x - 1 != 0  (in log(x)/(x - 1))
  requires x > 0
  defined on (numerically, grid of 20001 over [-10, 10]):
    x in (0, 1)
    x in (1, 10]
    except x ≈ 1  (x - 1 = 0)
> continuity sin(1/x)
continuity of sin(1/x):
  undefined at x = 0 (the ends located numerically)
  x = 0: essential (no limit: f oscillates)
  proved: built from operations that are continuous where defined, so it is continuous on its domain; points outside it located numerically; x has no range: searched [-10, 10]
> continuity x*sin(1/x)
continuity of x*sin(1/x):
  undefined at x = 0 (the ends located numerically)
  x = 0: removable: both limits are -2.094083075e-15, f is undefined there
  proved: built from operations that are continuous where defined, so it is continuous on its domain; points outside it located numerically; x has no range: searched [-10, 10]
> discontinuities 1/x
discontinuities of 1/x:
  undefined at x = 0 (the ends located numerically)
  x = 0: infinite (a pole): -inf from the left, +inf from the right
  proved: built from operations that are continuous where defined, so it is continuous on its domain; points outside it located numerically; x has no range: searched [-10, 10]
> domain 1/(x^2-1)
domain of 1/(x^2 - 1):
  x: real (default [-10, 10])
  requires x^2 - 1 != 0  (in 1/(x^2 - 1))
  defined on (numerically, grid of 20001 over [-10, 10]):
    x in [-10, -1)
    x in (-1, 1)
    x in (1, 10]
    except x ≈ -1  (x^2 - 1 = 0)
    except x ≈ 1  (x^2 - 1 = 0)
